WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:13.870 --> 00:00:18.210
Hi and welcome back to another episode
with ItsDrDan and this time we're

00:00:18.210 --> 00:00:20.230
going to be looking into gas laws.

00:00:20.620 --> 00:00:25.639
So last time on our previous video
of introduction to gases and kinetic

00:00:25.639 --> 00:00:29.750
molecular theory we learned that we
have little gas or little particles that

00:00:29.750 --> 00:00:34.025
are bouncing all over the place moving
in constant speed motion and they're

00:00:34.025 --> 00:00:39.575
applying pressures with inside of whatever
space they are located in they fill

00:00:39.595 --> 00:00:41.885
whatever volume they are currently in.

00:00:42.255 --> 00:00:46.214
So for example the air in your room,
it's in every square inch of your room.

00:00:46.525 --> 00:00:48.925
It's not just in one little
location around our mouth.

00:00:48.925 --> 00:00:53.834
It is everywhere then that's because all
those millions and millions of atoms that

00:00:53.834 --> 00:00:59.129
are all around are all bouncing around
and moving and flowing, so There are

00:00:59.129 --> 00:01:04.140
a lot of very simple laws, being that
these are ideal gases, talking about very

00:01:04.140 --> 00:01:06.380
minimal interactions between each other.

00:01:06.710 --> 00:01:10.150
We can describe them with laws,
meaning that we can use a lot of

00:01:10.150 --> 00:01:14.329
mathematics to describe how can we
predict how they're going to function.

00:01:14.919 --> 00:01:17.730
So, one of the first ones is Boyle's Law.

00:01:18.180 --> 00:01:22.650
So, what Boyle's Law tells us is that
there is, whenever you have a pressure

00:01:22.680 --> 00:01:25.949
and a volume of a gas, that are initial.

00:01:25.960 --> 00:01:29.750
So whenever we have something that's
considered to be initial, we're

00:01:29.750 --> 00:01:33.000
going to be representing that with,
so if I have an initial pressure,

00:01:33.020 --> 00:01:34.460
that's going to be with a one.

00:01:34.810 --> 00:01:36.920
If it's an initial
volume, that would be V1.

00:01:38.159 --> 00:01:42.869
So whenever you have those two values,
they are going to be equal to a

00:01:42.880 --> 00:01:45.000
final pressure and a final volume.

00:01:46.215 --> 00:01:51.155
of that gas, meaning that let's say if I
took a, a balloon and I'm holding it in

00:01:51.165 --> 00:01:55.225
my hand, well, what were to happen if you
were to try to compress it in your hand?

00:01:55.435 --> 00:01:57.705
Is it going to resist you
or is it going to pop?

00:01:57.985 --> 00:02:01.605
Well, if while you're pressing on
that balloon, you're going to feel it

00:02:01.605 --> 00:02:05.885
resisting you until eventually it pops,
but for the longest time it will resist.

00:02:06.185 --> 00:02:07.175
That's pressure.

00:02:07.625 --> 00:02:12.715
So the whole idea that is, okay, is that
there's this inverse relationship here,

00:02:12.874 --> 00:02:20.085
meaning that as you increase pressure,
the volume of which that gas is occupying

00:02:20.385 --> 00:02:22.885
is going to decrease as a result.

00:02:23.355 --> 00:02:25.714
So we can make a lot of predictions here.

00:02:25.714 --> 00:02:30.614
So as pressure increases, going from
P1 to P2, volume is going to decrease.

00:02:31.134 --> 00:02:38.005
If you go from P, uh, P1 to P2, so
initial to final, and it's decreasing.

00:02:38.684 --> 00:02:41.445
The volume is going to be
increasing as a result.

00:02:42.175 --> 00:02:44.565
So what does that look
like in terms of graphing?

00:02:45.094 --> 00:02:49.684
Well, if you have, let's say, looking
at this, if we have our initial pressure

00:02:50.374 --> 00:02:55.964
at initial volume, and let's say I
decrease the pressure and I go down,

00:02:56.464 --> 00:03:01.584
so if I'm going downwards, well, as
that goes down, well, what's happening

00:03:01.584 --> 00:03:06.975
is, well, the volume It's going to be
decreasing, it's going to be increasing.

00:03:06.995 --> 00:03:10.495
So volume goes up as pressure goes down.

00:03:10.945 --> 00:03:13.864
So it's all about that
inverse relationship.

00:03:13.885 --> 00:03:17.944
So how can we, what exactly are you
going to have to solve mathematically?

00:03:18.365 --> 00:03:21.885
Well, it's generally
pretty easy questions.

00:03:22.075 --> 00:03:26.534
So if I have the following one and
I ask you this question here and

00:03:26.534 --> 00:03:31.065
I say that we have a sample of gas
and it's placed inside of a piston.

00:03:31.665 --> 00:03:32.164
Well, it...

00:03:32.385 --> 00:03:34.525
A piston essentially
is this metal cylinder.

00:03:34.525 --> 00:03:35.765
It's going to be pushing down.

00:03:35.775 --> 00:03:39.155
It's going to be compressing
all the gases like I have here.

00:03:39.665 --> 00:03:43.115
So if I have a little, a little piston
here, I have a gas inside of here.

00:03:43.115 --> 00:03:47.825
They're bouncing all around
and this occupies 425

00:03:47.825 --> 00:03:49.184
milliliters at one atmosphere.

00:03:49.304 --> 00:03:53.985
So let's try to highlight all
that important information.

00:03:53.995 --> 00:03:56.084
So we have 525 milliliters.

00:03:56.585 --> 00:03:58.465
And that's at one atmosphere.

00:03:59.025 --> 00:04:03.585
So, whenever you're reading these, what
we want to do is we want to write down

00:04:03.785 --> 00:04:06.324
all of our given pieces of information.

00:04:07.005 --> 00:04:09.445
So we have 425 milliliters.

00:04:10.010 --> 00:04:12.140
and that's at one atmosphere.

00:04:13.269 --> 00:04:17.659
So that tells you your V1 and
then your P1 of those two values.

00:04:18.169 --> 00:04:22.089
Now it mentions that the
gas piston is compressed.

00:04:22.099 --> 00:04:25.000
So you can see that, okay, it's
coming really close together.

00:04:25.000 --> 00:04:27.270
So it's like the example I
said, if I push on a balloon,

00:04:27.580 --> 00:04:28.679
what's going to be happening?

00:04:29.059 --> 00:04:34.815
So when that compresses, it says
that the pressure reaches 3.

00:04:34.855 --> 00:04:35.935
28 atmospheres.

00:04:35.945 --> 00:04:41.265
So that means that as this pressure
is changing, it goes from 3.

00:04:41.315 --> 00:04:42.635
28 atmospheres.

00:04:43.555 --> 00:04:47.894
So what you see is that
pressure is increasing.

00:04:48.274 --> 00:04:50.754
So, how can we try to
predict this problem?

00:04:50.754 --> 00:04:56.535
Well, going back up, well as
pressure increases, so looking at

00:04:56.545 --> 00:04:58.475
these, right, so pressure increases.

00:04:58.960 --> 00:05:00.710
Volume should decrease.

00:05:00.719 --> 00:05:04.000
So it's telling you that your final
answer should be a tinier volume.

00:05:04.320 --> 00:05:05.859
It's a check for yourself.

00:05:05.890 --> 00:05:08.960
Which makes it really nicer when
you're doing these problems.

00:05:09.369 --> 00:05:11.140
We like predictable problems.

00:05:11.140 --> 00:05:14.010
We don't want something that,
you know, it goes into something

00:05:14.010 --> 00:05:15.809
that doesn't fit anywhere.

00:05:16.269 --> 00:05:18.249
So we're trying to figure out V2.

00:05:18.739 --> 00:05:24.299
So what we're going to be doing
initially is setting up our formula.

00:05:26.320 --> 00:05:32.600
Meaning that I have P1
V1 is equal to P2 V2.

00:05:33.200 --> 00:05:36.480
So we are trying to solve for V2 here.

00:05:36.480 --> 00:05:38.680
So V2 is our unknown variable.

00:05:39.070 --> 00:05:41.990
So the whole idea here is
we need to get it by itself.

00:05:42.230 --> 00:05:43.889
Well, what's the easiest way to do that?

00:05:44.380 --> 00:05:50.920
Well, if I divide both sides by
P2, what that's going to cause

00:05:51.040 --> 00:05:55.110
is for the P2 values to cancel.

00:05:55.900 --> 00:06:02.140
And that will, in a sense, get
V1 by itself, so we have P1,

00:06:03.109 --> 00:06:09.260
P1 times V1, divided by P2, and
that's going to be equal to V2.

00:06:10.049 --> 00:06:13.990
So, all we now need to do is
plug in all of our information.

00:06:14.550 --> 00:06:17.730
Now, when you're plugging these
things in, whatever the common,

00:06:18.604 --> 00:06:20.574
Variable is so in this case pressure.

00:06:20.975 --> 00:06:22.515
They need to be in the same units.

00:06:22.515 --> 00:06:26.764
They need to be able to cancel each other
out So when we are doing this, right

00:06:26.785 --> 00:06:33.775
our initial pressure was one atmosphere
and that's going to be multiplied by

00:06:33.784 --> 00:06:42.655
425 milliliters And that's all going to
be divided by pressure 2, which is 3.

00:06:42.715 --> 00:06:43.945
28 atmospheres.

00:06:44.805 --> 00:06:49.805
Alright, so when we do that, what
that's going to be all equal to is

00:06:49.805 --> 00:06:56.795
that V2 will be equal to, um, 129.

00:06:56.815 --> 00:07:00.075
6 mL.

00:07:00.655 --> 00:07:02.165
Now, is that the correct sig figs?

00:07:02.365 --> 00:07:03.135
We're just going to round it.

00:07:03.175 --> 00:07:06.265
And you might notice there's three
sig figs for all of your givens.

00:07:06.845 --> 00:07:08.675
So this will round to 130.

00:07:10.109 --> 00:07:10.820
Milliliters.

00:07:11.179 --> 00:07:12.850
Okay, and now this would be your final.

00:07:14.460 --> 00:07:15.440
Your final answer there.

00:07:15.909 --> 00:07:17.420
So that's how we do Boyle's Law.

00:07:17.550 --> 00:07:20.980
For the next one, Charles Law.

00:07:22.100 --> 00:07:27.730
Charles Law is another type of
really, another really common gas law.

00:07:27.840 --> 00:07:32.450
So what this is telling you is that volume
is directly proportional to temperature.

00:07:33.145 --> 00:07:36.305
And this could kind of make some sense
if you think back to the simulation that

00:07:36.305 --> 00:07:41.745
I showed in my previous problem where
as we increase temperature, particles

00:07:41.755 --> 00:07:43.985
start to move at a very rapid pace.

00:07:44.485 --> 00:07:47.655
So when that happens, those
particles are going to be pushing

00:07:47.655 --> 00:07:51.584
and banging against all the walls
of whatever its container is.

00:07:51.594 --> 00:07:55.484
So if it's something that's flexible,
well, it's going to grow as a

00:07:55.484 --> 00:07:56.704
result, so they're proportional.

00:07:57.364 --> 00:08:02.104
So meaning that if, as initial volume,
if that's going to be increasing,

00:08:02.864 --> 00:08:06.880
then You're going to see the same,
a proportional change in your

00:08:06.880 --> 00:08:08.640
final temperature and final volume.

00:08:08.900 --> 00:08:13.799
So the way that we can look at this
is a very different graphical picture,

00:08:14.319 --> 00:08:20.909
is that if I have volume at an initial
temperature, and if I increase the volume,

00:08:21.415 --> 00:08:23.215
Temperature is going to increase too.

00:08:23.235 --> 00:08:25.015
They're both going to increase.

00:08:25.375 --> 00:08:28.195
So if temperature increases,
volume should increase too.

00:08:28.255 --> 00:08:29.984
They are directly proportional.

00:08:30.555 --> 00:08:33.424
Now, one of the things about this
is, being that we're talking about

00:08:33.504 --> 00:08:37.924
temperature, this is one of the units
that needs to be in the correct scale.

00:08:38.314 --> 00:08:39.804
It needs to be in Kelvin.

00:08:40.205 --> 00:08:45.335
Now, the reason behind that is because
Kelvin is an absolute unit scale.

00:08:45.845 --> 00:08:49.525
Meaning that it only
refers to positive values.

00:08:49.564 --> 00:08:54.765
You cannot do the mathematics here with
negatives or with zero as a possibility.

00:08:54.775 --> 00:08:56.674
It has to be a positive number.

00:08:56.674 --> 00:08:59.885
So anywhere from 0.

00:08:59.885 --> 00:09:02.705
0001 all the way up to infinity, right?

00:09:02.715 --> 00:09:04.475
So it's always a positive value.

00:09:05.275 --> 00:09:07.395
So, now this is also predictable.

00:09:07.944 --> 00:09:10.694
As volume increases,
temperature should increase.

00:09:10.875 --> 00:09:11.954
If volume decreases...

00:09:12.245 --> 00:09:13.405
Temperature should decrease.

00:09:13.415 --> 00:09:17.785
So we should be able to predict our answer
too, which is always a nice thing to have.

00:09:17.785 --> 00:09:21.505
We want as predictable as possible.

00:09:22.755 --> 00:09:26.664
So the idea for this one, if
I had the following question,

00:09:27.314 --> 00:09:30.234
so if I say I have a 2.

00:09:30.235 --> 00:09:34.774
75 liter sample, okay, so we
have a volume, and it's at a

00:09:34.775 --> 00:09:37.255
temperature of 25 degrees C.

00:09:38.155 --> 00:09:42.205
When the amount of gas and pressure
are held constant, so being that that's

00:09:42.205 --> 00:09:45.465
telling you that it's not relevant to
this problem It's helping you be able

00:09:45.465 --> 00:09:49.565
to decide which formula to use this
time We know it's Charles law, but most

00:09:49.574 --> 00:09:55.424
problems are given you have to pick
from all of them What is the new volume

00:09:55.755 --> 00:09:59.425
when argon is heated to 45 degrees C?

00:09:59.775 --> 00:10:05.215
So argon is being heated this Volume of
gas is going to be growing because they're

00:10:05.215 --> 00:10:12.270
moving more rapidly so Now keep note, we
do have to convert all these to Kelvin.

00:10:12.390 --> 00:10:14.370
So we have to use our
little Kelvin equation.

00:10:14.790 --> 00:10:17.680
So why don't we write down
all these values we had.

00:10:18.120 --> 00:10:23.069
So we had that volume 1 was 2.

00:10:23.070 --> 00:10:24.379
75 liters.

00:10:24.820 --> 00:10:29.860
We have, we don't have a
volume 2, kind of going through

00:10:29.860 --> 00:10:31.700
these, so we have no volume 2.

00:10:31.720 --> 00:10:32.710
That's a question mark.

00:10:33.725 --> 00:10:41.545
And T1 and T2 were 25
degrees C and 45 degrees C.

00:10:42.935 --> 00:10:45.705
Alright, so when you're going through
these, the first thing we have to do

00:10:45.705 --> 00:10:47.914
is convert our temperatures to Kelvin.

00:10:48.435 --> 00:10:52.105
So in order to do that, we
just take our temperatures.

00:10:52.105 --> 00:10:56.105
So if I have T1, Kelvin is
just going to be that 25.

00:10:56.625 --> 00:11:00.155
0 degrees C plus 273.

00:11:00.975 --> 00:11:04.105
And what that's going to give
us, our first value is 298.

00:11:05.579 --> 00:11:12.380
Kelvin for T1, and then T2, doing the
same mathematics, you're going to see

00:11:12.380 --> 00:11:15.669
that that one's going to be 318 Kelvin.

00:11:16.709 --> 00:11:19.689
All right, so now we can
plug it into our equation.

00:11:19.789 --> 00:11:28.260
Our equation before was V1,
T1, is equal to V2 over T2.

00:11:29.320 --> 00:11:35.540
So, what we're going to do is we're going
to rearrange it to get V2 by itself.

00:11:35.990 --> 00:11:40.780
So what we can simply do is, being
that we want to get this by itself,

00:11:41.190 --> 00:11:44.200
we can multiply both sides by T2.

00:11:45.950 --> 00:11:51.099
This is going to cause the number of the
T2s on the right hand side to cancel,

00:11:52.000 --> 00:12:00.340
giving us a new formula, or a rearranged
formula, of this is equal to V2.

00:12:01.275 --> 00:12:05.075
And what we're simply going to be
doing is plugging in our variables.

00:12:05.625 --> 00:12:12.565
So we have T2, which
was equal to 318 Kelvin.

00:12:14.104 --> 00:12:16.574
We have V1, which was 2.

00:12:16.574 --> 00:12:18.704
75 liters.

00:12:20.215 --> 00:12:24.965
And then we have T1, which was 298 Kelvin.

00:12:25.625 --> 00:12:29.654
Now, notice how the Kelvin ends
up canceling each other out,

00:12:29.665 --> 00:12:31.425
leaving us only with a volume.

00:12:32.085 --> 00:12:37.784
So when we go through this calculation,
what we're going to end up getting is 2.

00:12:38.725 --> 00:12:42.015
93 liters of gas.

00:12:42.485 --> 00:12:44.765
Now, does that make sense
based on what we saw?

00:12:45.205 --> 00:12:51.085
Well, as you notice, temperature was
increasing as we went from T1 to T2.

00:12:52.149 --> 00:12:56.609
So, if that's the case, volume should
also increase, and based on our original

00:12:56.609 --> 00:13:00.369
volume, you can see that increase by a
little tiny bit when it was heated up.

00:13:00.780 --> 00:13:05.229
So this gives you an idea of what we
are looking for with this gas law.

00:13:05.339 --> 00:13:09.740
So as volume increases,
temperature should also increase.

00:13:09.930 --> 00:13:11.780
Same thing with vice versa.

00:13:11.920 --> 00:13:14.310
Temperature decreases,
volume should decrease.

00:13:17.470 --> 00:13:21.900
The next law, so there's a lot of these,
but we'll go through them step by step.

00:13:22.129 --> 00:13:24.449
This one is Gay Lussac's Law.

00:13:25.050 --> 00:13:27.310
This one's also a very similar idea.

00:13:27.890 --> 00:13:29.960
This one tells you that as pressure.

00:13:30.439 --> 00:13:32.360
is directly proportional to temperature.

00:13:32.510 --> 00:13:36.990
So if you think of your car tires,
for example, throughout the summer, as

00:13:36.990 --> 00:13:40.079
it gets really hot out, the pressure
of your tires is going to increase.

00:13:40.139 --> 00:13:44.139
This is one of the reasons why your
tires have a maximum recommended

00:13:44.139 --> 00:13:49.109
pressure on your tires, because
if you overfill them, and then it

00:13:49.109 --> 00:13:50.430
gets hot out, they will explode.

00:13:50.995 --> 00:13:54.595
Versus when it gets to winter time and
you might have that air pressure light

00:13:54.595 --> 00:13:56.145
that comes on saying low pressure.

00:13:56.405 --> 00:13:58.965
Well, that's because all those
molecules are getting really cold

00:13:58.995 --> 00:14:03.185
and they're starting to kind of
try to bundle up really closely.

00:14:03.755 --> 00:14:08.044
So whenever you have a gas at initial
pressure and initial temperature,

00:14:08.104 --> 00:14:12.274
this is proportional to the final
pressure and final temperature.

00:14:12.974 --> 00:14:15.394
So it's a very similar graph
to the one I just showed you.

00:14:15.674 --> 00:14:19.814
Instead though, when we have an initial
pressure and temperature and if I decide

00:14:19.814 --> 00:14:25.440
to increase Pressure, temperature is going
to increase as well and it's the whole

00:14:25.440 --> 00:14:30.130
idea that fast moving molecule picture the
faster They're moving if you have a solid

00:14:30.130 --> 00:14:35.700
container that can't expand Let's say for
example, maybe a gas cylinder the steel

00:14:35.700 --> 00:14:40.599
walls Well, if those molecules are heated
up, they're going to be really pushing

00:14:40.599 --> 00:14:46.520
against those walls of that container So
once again, this is a direct relationship

00:14:46.540 --> 00:14:48.535
meaning that They go together.

00:14:49.075 --> 00:14:51.705
As pressure increases,
temperature increases.

00:14:51.805 --> 00:14:53.295
They both increase together.

00:14:53.644 --> 00:14:56.324
Or, the opposite is they
both decrease together.

00:14:56.765 --> 00:14:59.235
Pressure goes down,
temperature comes down too.

00:15:00.635 --> 00:15:02.545
So, what kind of problems would you have?

